# Screenshot automation

To update screenshots used in our documentation, we script shot-scraper to grab
screenshots of small chunks of our UI. This uses a QA user with minimal
permissions to projects that are benign enough to exist in public documentation.
For any place we deal with sensitive information, shot-scraper can use DOM
manipulation to replace the sensitive content.

To run, simply run:

    % make

This runs the following steps:

- Creates the auth files that will contain the session information so that
  following screenshots use an authenticated request.
- Runs shot-scraper against the YAML configuration files with all the
  screenshots to generate, writing them into docs/user.

Creating the auth file will open a browser so you can log in. Log in as the
directed user. This will create a file that will be reused by shot-scraper to
generate authenticated requests to the production dashboard.

Review the result with `git diff`. Use `make community` or `make business` to
run only one of them, and `OUT=/some/dir` to write the images somewhere else.

## Features that aren't deployed yet

Production only shows what's deployed. To take screenshots of a new feature,
use your local development instance instead:

    % make ENV=dev community

Log in with your local user when asked. dev.env lists the projects and objects
the screenshots borrow from your database: update it to match your data.

## Adding a screenshot

Add an entry to community.yml or business.yml. Placeholders like
`${COMMUNITY_URL}` and `${PROJECT}` come from prod.env and dev.env. Start the
entry's `javascript` with `${HELPERS}`: it hides notifications and staff-only
items, shows projects as "docs", and defines helpers (see helpers.js). Then
choose the area with one of its crop helpers, like `mainColumn()`, and use
`selector: "#shot"`:

    - url: ${COMMUNITY_URL}/dashboard/${PROJECT}/domains/
      javascript: |-
        ${HELPERS}
        mainColumn();
      selector: "#shot"
      output: ${OUT}/img/screenshot_domains.png

Never publish real usernames, secrets, or development URLs: replace them in the
`javascript` step. Refer to the existing screenshots and the shot-scraper
documentation for usage and help configuring.
